      modelAnswer: 'Big O notation describes how the running time or memory use of an algorithm grows as the input size n becomes large, ignoring constant factors and lower-order terms. It captures the worst-case asymptotic rate of growth. For example, an algorithm that takes 3n² + 5n + 17 microseconds is described as O(n²) because, for large enough n, the n² term dominates. Common classes from fastest-growing to slowest are O(1) constant, O(log n) logarithmic, O(n) linear, O(n log n), O(n²) quadratic, O(2ⁿ) exponential and O(n!) factorial. Big O lets programmers compare algorithms independently of hardware: a binary search at O(log n) is asymptotically faster than a linear search at O(n), and the gap widens dramatically as n grows. It is not, however, a precise measurement: for small n an O(n²) algorithm can be faster than an O(n log n) one, because the constants and lower-order terms hidden by Big O may dominate. Big O therefore guides design decisions for scalable problems but does not replace empirical measurement on real data.'
