      modelAnswer: 'Both are divide-and-conquer sorting algorithms with O(n log n) average time, but they differ in mechanism, worst case, memory use and practical speed. Merge sort splits the list in half (an effortless division), recursively sorts both halves, then performs a non-trivial merge step to combine them; the work happens during combination. Quick sort selects a pivot element and partitions the list so that all smaller elements lie to its left and all larger to its right (the work is in the partition); the two halves are then recursively sorted, and the combination step is trivial because the partitions are already correctly ordered relative to one another. Time complexity. Merge sort is O(n log n) in best, average and worst cases — its splitting is always balanced. Quick sort is O(n log n) on average but O(n²) in the worst case when the pivot consistently happens to be the smallest or largest element, producing maximally unbalanced partitions. Modern implementations choose pivots cleverly (median-of-three, randomised) and switch to insertion sort for small partitions, making O(n²) cases extremely rare in practice. Space. Quick sort sorts in place with only O(log n) stack frames for recursion. Merge sort needs O(n) auxiliary space for the merge buffer. On memory-constrained systems quick sort is preferred for this reason. Stability. Merge sort is stable; quick sort is not by default (although stable variants exist). Cache behaviour. Quick sort\'s partitioning is highly cache-friendly because it works in contiguous regions of the array; merge sort\'s merge step shuffles between two halves and an auxiliary buffer, hurting locality. This is why quick sort is typically the fastest general-purpose sort on modern hardware, despite its worse asymptotic worst case. Use cases. Merge sort wins where worst-case performance must be guaranteed, where data does not fit in memory (external sorting), and where stability is required. Quick sort wins as the default in-memory sort on arrays of primitive types. Real systems often use hybrid algorithms — Timsort (used in Python and Java) merges runs of already-sorted data; introsort (used in C++) starts with quick sort and falls back to heap sort if recursion goes too deep, combining quick sort\'s speed with a guaranteed O(n log n) upper bound.'
